const flip=(title,cards)=>({t:'w',kind:'flip',title,cards});
const kase=(title,nodes)=>({t:'w',kind:'case',title,start:'a',nodes});
const chk=(q,options,why)=>({t:'check',q,options,why});
const ex=(title,prompt,steps,answer)=>({t:'example',title,prompt,steps,answer});

export const MI1={title:'The event and what judges reward',hook:'Every medical device, app and care process started as someone’s idea about a problem worth solving. Medical Innovation challenges teams of two to four to invent an original solution, build a prototype, plan an exhibit and convince judges that it could improve health care. Knowing the structure and scoring from the start lets you design your project to earn every point.',objectives:['Describe the event’s rounds, display time and scoring.','Explain what makes an innovation eligible and original.','Identify the four questions every exhibit must answer.','Plan a year-long project timeline.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Medical Innovation is a **team event for 2–4 members** in the **secondary** and **postsecondary/collegiate** divisions. Teams create an **original medical innovation**, **build a prototype**, provide **evidence** for why it is needed and plan an **exhibit**.',
'**Round One** is a pre-judged **digital outline**—slide style, **no more than 13 slides** plus references—uploaded by one member as **one PDF** by **May 15**; it is worth **110 points**. Round One is judged in early June, and finalists are determined then, although results are released after the display time. At ILC, all teams attend the **Required Project Display Time** (missing it costs **15 points** from Round Two). Finalists give a **five-minute presentation** with their exhibit, worth **130 points**.',
'Notecards are allowed (electronic notes are not shown), presenters may **pick up display items** and all members must take an active role. Proper dress earns bonus points.',
{t:'table',caption:'Event structure',head:['Part','What you do','Points'],rows:[['Round One','Digital outline (≤13 slides + references)','110'],['Display Time','Stand with your exhibit at ILC','−15 if missed'],['Round Two','5-minute presentation with exhibit and prototype','130']]}]],
['What counts as a medical innovation',[
'The innovation must be **original**—your team’s own idea and design—and something that could **advance medicine or the delivery of health care**. Topics may include medical or health care innovations, emerging technologies in health and advances in medicine: a device, a tool, an app concept, a system or a process.',
'It is the team’s responsibility to do **due diligence** to make sure the idea does not already exist in publication or patent, starting with an internet search and continuing with patent databases (USPTO or the European Patent Office). HOSA does **not** provide patent protection; if you want a patent, that is your responsibility.',
'The Round One rubric awards **15 points** for an original innovation—and 0 if it is not original.',
"Innovations do not have to be high-tech. A simple tool, a redesigned form or a new workflow can be just as valuable as an electronic device if it solves a real problem in a new way.",
sort('Eligible innovation idea?',['Likely eligible (if original)','Not eligible'],[['A new device to help stroke patients grip utensils',0],['Rebuilding an existing commercial glucose meter',1],['A system to reduce missed medication doses in nursing homes',0],['A copied app with a new logo',1],['A low-cost tool for safer IV line labeling',0]])]],
['Four questions every exhibit answers',[
'The guidelines list the information the exhibit must cover: **what the innovation is** and what it does or how it is used; its **impact on the future of health care delivery**; how it may **increase quality of life**; and how it may **reduce health care costs**.',
'Anyone viewing the exhibit should understand the innovation **without someone there to explain it**. That standard shapes how you design both the outline and the exhibit.',
'These four questions also become the main content slides of your digital outline.',
flip('The four exhibit questions',[['What is it?','What it does and how it is used'],['Future of health care','Impact on how care is delivered'],['Quality of life','How it improves patients’ lives'],['Costs','How it may reduce health care costs'],['Stand-alone test','Viewers understand without a presenter'],['Originality','Your own idea and design']])]],
['How judges score',[
'Round One scores the **title slide** (5); the **description of the innovation and how it is used**, **impact on the future of health care delivery**, **quality of life**, **reducing health care costs** and **creative and artistic impact** (15 each); **organization**, **reference page(s)** and **total slides** (5 each); and **original medical innovation** (15).',
'Round Two scores **presentation content**—explain and teach (15), demonstration of the prototype (15), purpose, value and benefit (15), cost (5), training requirements (5) and career implications (5)—plus **delivery** (voice, stage presence, diction, team participation, organization and flow and exhibit incorporated) and **exhibit visuals** (artistic design, creativity and originality and overall innovation at 10 each, and safety at 5).',
'Notice that the prototype demonstration is one of the highest-value items in Round Two.',
match('Rubric item and round',[['Original medical innovation','Round One (15)'],['Total slides ≤13','Round One (5)'],['Demonstration of prototype','Round Two (15)'],['Training requirements','Round Two (5)'],['Safety of exhibit','Round Two (5)'],['Creative and artistic impact','Round One (15)']])]],
['A year-long plan',[
'**Early fall:** form the team and find a real problem through research, observation and interviews. **Fall:** search for existing solutions and patents, brainstorm and select an original idea. **Winter:** design and build prototypes, test and improve them and gather evidence on need, impact and cost. **Early spring:** build the exhibit and the 13-slide outline; upload one PDF well before **May 15**. **Late spring:** rehearse the presentation and demonstration and prepare for the display time.',
'Keep an **engineering notebook** or shared log with dates, sketches, test results and decisions. It helps prove originality and makes the outline and presentation easier to build.',
'Check your state conference rules; if your state uses the HOSA upload system, upload again for ILC.',
order('Medical Innovation timeline',['Identify a real health care problem','Search for existing solutions and patents','Brainstorm and select an original idea','Design, prototype and test','Gather evidence on impact and cost','Build the exhibit and upload the outline by May 15','Rehearse the presentation and demo'])]],
['Team roles',[
'With two to four members, divide roles while keeping everyone involved: a **research lead** (need, evidence, patents), a **design lead** (sketches, prototype), a **business lead** (costs, implementation, careers) and a **communications lead** (outline, exhibit, presentation). Everyone should be able to explain the whole innovation.',
'Hold short weekly meetings to review progress and decide next steps. All members must take an active role in Round Two, so plan speaking parts early.',
"Decide early how the team will make decisions and resolve disagreements, such as voting or using a decision matrix. Clear agreements keep the project moving when deadlines approach.",
chk('Why should every member understand the whole innovation?',['All members must take an active role, and judges may ask anyone','Only one member will speak','Roles are not allowed','The exhibit explains everything, so presenters do not need to'],'Shared knowledge supports team participation.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Starting the project',{a:{text:'Your team loves the idea of a “smart pill bottle.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do first?',choices:[['Search for existing products and patents to see whether the idea is original','b','Correct.',true],['Start building immediately','b','Check originality before investing time.',false]]},b:{text:'You find several smart pill bottles already exist. What next?',choices:[['Look for an unmet need they do not solve and design something new','c','Correct.',true],['Copy one and add your logo','c','The innovation must be original.',false]]},c:{text:'You redirect toward an unmet need you discovered in your research.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Teams of 2–4 (secondary and postsecondary) create an original innovation with a prototype; Round One is a pre-judged digital outline of ≤13 slides plus references (one PDF by May 15, 110 points); ILC display time is required (−15 if missed); Round Two is a 5-minute presentation (130 points).','The innovation must be original and could advance medicine or health care delivery; teams must search publications and patents (USPTO, EPO); HOSA does not provide patent protection.','The exhibit must explain what the innovation is and how it is used, its impact on health care delivery, quality of life and cost reduction, and stand alone without a presenter; plan with a timeline, a notebook and clear team roles.'],terms:[['Medical innovation','A new device, system or process that could advance medicine or health care delivery.'],['Prototype','An early model built to test and demonstrate an idea.'],['Due diligence','Careful research to confirm an idea is original.']],mcqs:[q('How many members may be on a Medical Innovation team?','2–4','1','5–6','Exactly 4','Team size.'),q('What is the maximum number of outline slides, not counting references?','13','16','20','10','Slide limit.'),q('How many points is Round One worth?','110','130','240','50','Outline.'),q('What happens if a team misses the Required Display Time?','A 15-point deduction from Round Two','Nothing','Disqualification from all events','Bonus points','Rule.'),q('Who is responsible for checking that the idea is original?','The competitors','HOSA','The judges','The patent office automatically','Due diligence.'),q('Does HOSA provide patent protection?','No','Yes','Only for winners','Only in Round Two','Disclaimer.'),q('Which Round Two item is worth 15 points?','Demonstration of prototype','Cost','Training requirements','Safety','Content.'),q('Anyone viewing the exhibit should be able to:','Understand the innovation without a presenter','Buy the product','Use it on themselves','Download it','Stand-alone.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Medical Innovation event, what makes an innovation eligible and how your team would plan the project.',rubric:[['Event',['2-4','outline','13 slides','pdf','may 15','display time','5 minutes']],['Eligibility',['original','advance','health care','patent','due diligence','uspto']],['Exhibit questions',['what it is','used','future','quality of life','costs','stand alone']],['Planning',['timeline','prototype','notebook','roles','evidence','test']]],model:'Medical Innovation is a team event for two to four secondary or postsecondary members who create an original medical innovation, build a prototype and plan an exhibit. In Round One, judges score a pre-judged digital outline in slide style, no more than 13 slides plus references, uploaded by one member as a single PDF by May 15, for 110 points. At ILC, every team must attend the Required Project Display Time or lose 15 points, and finalists give a five-minute presentation with their exhibit and prototype worth 130 points. Our innovation must be our own idea and design and something that could advance medicine or health care delivery, and it is our responsibility to search the internet, publications and patent databases such as USPTO and the European Patent Office to make sure it does not already exist, since the original innovation item is worth 15 points and HOSA does not provide patent protection. Our exhibit and outline must explain what the innovation is and how it is used, its impact on the future of health care delivery, how it may improve quality of life and how it may reduce health care costs, clearly enough that a viewer understands without us. We would plan the year: find a real problem in early fall, search for existing solutions, brainstorm and select an original idea, prototype and test through winter while gathering evidence, then build the exhibit and upload the outline well before May 15 and rehearse. We would keep a dated engineering notebook and assign research, design, business and communications roles while making sure everyone understands the whole project.'},sources:[RULES,USPTO]};

export const MI2={title:'Finding a need worth solving',hook:'The best innovations do not start with a gadget—they start with a problem. Judges score the purpose of your innovation, why it is needed and how it adds value, so your first job is to find a real, important health care need and understand it deeply. Design thinking begins with empathy for the people who live with the problem.',objectives:['Use observation, interviews and research to identify health care needs.','Write a clear need statement.','Evaluate needs by size, severity and feasibility.','Gather evidence that a need is real.'],sections:[
['Where needs come from',[
'Needs appear wherever patients, families or health workers struggle: **errors** (medication mix-ups, falls, infections), **inefficiencies** (long waits, repeated tests, paperwork), **access barriers** (rural distance, cost, disability), **discomfort or fear** (painful procedures, anxiety) and **gaps in monitoring** (missed warning signs at home).',
'Sources for discovering needs include **observation** (with permission) in clinics, nursing homes or pharmacies; **interviews** with health workers, patients and caregivers; **news and research** on health problems; and your own and your family’s experiences.',
'Health workers are especially good sources because they see the same problems every day.',
"Professional journals, hospital quality reports and patient safety organizations also publish lists of common problems, such as medication errors and hospital-acquired infections. These lists can point you toward needs that matter across many facilities.",
sort('Type of need',['Safety','Access','Efficiency'],[['Patients falling when getting out of bed at night',0],['Long drives to see a specialist',1],['Nurses spending time searching for supplies',2],['Wrong medication given to the wrong patient',0],['Wheelchair users unable to reach a clinic counter',1],['Duplicate paperwork at every visit',2]])]],
['Empathy and design thinking',[
'**Design thinking** is a human-centered approach that typically moves through **empathize**, **define**, **ideate**, **prototype** and **test**. The first stage—**empathy**—means understanding the experience of the people affected: their goals, frustrations, environments and workarounds.',
'Ask open-ended questions such as “Walk me through what happens when…” and “What is the hardest part?” Observe what people do, not just what they say. Note **workarounds**—tape, sticky notes or improvised tools often reveal unmet needs.',
'Respect privacy: never record patient information, and follow facility rules.',
"Interview several people with different roles. A nurse, a patient and a family caregiver may describe the same problem very differently, and the overlap among their stories often reveals the most important need.",
order('Design thinking stages',['Empathize','Define','Ideate','Prototype','Test'])]],
['Writing a need statement',[
'A **need statement** describes the problem without proposing a solution. A useful format: **“A way to [outcome] for [population] who [problem or situation].”** For example: “A way to reduce nighttime falls for older adults in nursing homes who get up to use the bathroom.”',
'Keep the solution out of the statement—“a smart bed alarm” is a solution; “a way to reduce nighttime falls” is a need. A solution-free statement keeps you open to better ideas.',
'A clear need statement guides research, brainstorming and the “purpose” part of your presentation.',
"Write several versions of the need statement and test them with the people you interviewed. If they say, “Yes, that is exactly the problem,” you have defined it well.",
sort('Need statement or solution?',['Need statement','Solution'],[['A way to reduce missed doses for older adults who take many medicines',0],['A smartphone app with reminders',1],['A way to help children with asthma use inhalers correctly',0],['A glowing inhaler spacer',1],['A way to speed up triage in crowded emergency departments',0]])]],
['Evaluating needs',[
'Not every need is a good project. Evaluate each candidate by **size** (how many people are affected), **severity** (how serious the consequences are), **current solutions** (are existing options inadequate?), **feasibility** (can your team prototype something meaningful?) and **impact potential** (on quality of life and costs).',
'Score candidate needs in a simple table and choose one that is important, under-solved and realistic for a student team to prototype safely.',
"Feasibility matters for a student team. A need that requires surgery, drugs or living tissue to test is unrealistic for a safe exhibit, while a need related to devices, tools, information or workflow is usually easier to prototype.",
ex('Scoring candidate needs','Score two needs from 1 (low) to 5 (high) on four criteria (scores invented for practice).',['Nighttime falls in nursing homes: size 4, severity 5, gap 3, feasibility 4 = 16','Rare surgical instrument problem: size 1, severity 4, gap 4, feasibility 1 = 10','Higher total suggests the stronger project','Discuss the scores as a team before deciding'],'Nighttime falls scores higher (16 vs. 10) (scores invented for practice).')]],
['Gathering evidence of need',[
'Judges want **supporting evidence** for why your innovation is needed. Use **statistics** from reliable sources (CDC, NIH, WHO, peer-reviewed research) on how common and costly the problem is; **quotes** from health workers or patients you interviewed; and **observations** you made.',
'Combine numbers and stories: a statistic shows scale, while a quote shows human impact. Cite every source on your reference slides.',
'Evidence of need also supports your claims about quality of life and costs later.',
"Keep a separate evidence file with every statistic, quote and observation, including its source and date. When you build the outline and presentation, you can choose the strongest pieces quickly.",
match('Evidence type and example',[['Statistic','Annual number of older adults treated for falls'],['Interview quote','A nurse describing nighttime fall risks'],['Observation','Noting dim hallways during a facility visit'],['Research study','A peer-reviewed study on fall prevention'],['Cost data','Average cost of a fall-related hospitalization'],['Existing product review','Limitations of current bed alarms']])]],
['Understanding the users',[
'Identify everyone who will interact with your innovation: the **primary user** (who uses it), the **patient** (who benefits), the **purchaser** (who pays—a hospital, insurer or consumer) and others such as caregivers or technicians. Their needs may differ.',
'For example, a fall-prevention device must be comfortable for residents, easy for nurses to set up and affordable for facilities. Listing users now helps with training requirements and career implications in Round Two.',
"Map the user journey from start to finish—before, during and after the problem occurs. A journey map often shows a moment where a small change could make a big difference.",
chk('For a hospital device, who is often the purchaser?',['The hospital or health system','Only the patient','The HOSA judges','The device’s designer'],'Different stakeholders have different needs.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Finding the need',{a:{text:'During a job shadow, you notice nurses tape handwritten labels on IV lines. (Scenario invented for practice.) What might this workaround reveal?',choices:[['An unmet need for clearer, safer IV line identification','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing important','b','Workarounds often reveal needs.',false]]},b:{text:'How would you write the need statement?',choices:[['“A way to help nurses quickly and accurately identify IV lines for patients with multiple infusions.”','c','Correct.',true],['“A color-coded smart IV tag.”','c','That is a solution, not a need.',false]]},c:{text:'You begin gathering evidence on IV line errors.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Needs come from errors, inefficiencies, access barriers, discomfort and monitoring gaps; discover them through observation, interviews, research and experience, and watch for workarounds.','Design thinking: empathize, define, ideate, prototype, test; write solution-free need statements (“A way to [outcome] for [population] who [problem]”).','Evaluate needs by size, severity, gaps in current solutions, feasibility and impact; gather statistics, quotes and observations as evidence; identify users, patients and purchasers.'],terms:[['Need statement','A solution-free description of a problem, population and desired outcome.'],['Design thinking','A human-centered process: empathize, define, ideate, prototype and test.'],['Workaround','An improvised fix that reveals an unmet need.']],mcqs:[q('What is the first stage of design thinking?','Empathize','Prototype','Test','Ideate','Human-centered.'),q('Which is a need statement?','A way to reduce nighttime falls for older adults','A smart bed alarm','A new app','A glowing bracelet','Solution-free.'),q('What do workarounds often reveal?','Unmet needs','Perfect systems','Nothing','Legal issues only','Clues.'),q('Which criterion asks how many people are affected?','Size','Severity','Feasibility','Color','Evaluation.'),q('Which is strong evidence of need?','Statistics from the CDC on fall injuries','A guess','A social media rumor','A cartoon','Reliable data.'),q('Who often pays for hospital devices?','The hospital or health system','The judges','The designer','Nobody','Purchaser.'),q('Which interview question is open-ended?','“Walk me through what happens when…”','“Do you like your job?”','“Is it bad?”','“Yes or no?”','Open.'),q('Why keep solutions out of need statements?','To stay open to better ideas','Because solutions are illegal','To save words','To confuse judges','Open thinking.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would find and define a health care need for Medical Innovation and show that it is real.',rubric:[['Discovery',['observation','interviews','research','experience','workarounds','errors']],['Empathy and definition',['empathize','design thinking','need statement','population','outcome','solution-free']],['Evaluation',['size','severity','current solutions','feasibility','impact']],['Evidence and users',['statistics','quotes','cite','users','patients','purchaser']]],model:'We would look for needs where patients or health workers struggle—errors, inefficiencies, access barriers, discomfort and monitoring gaps. With our advisor’s help, we would interview nurses at a local nursing home and, with permission, observe how residents move around at night, asking open-ended questions such as “Walk me through what happens when a resident gets up at night.” Following design thinking, this empathy stage would help us understand residents’ and nurses’ frustrations and the workarounds they use. We might find that residents often fall on the way to the bathroom in dim rooms. We would write a solution-free need statement: “A way to reduce nighttime falls for older adults in nursing homes who get up to use the bathroom.” We would compare this with other candidate needs using a scoring table for size, severity, gaps in current solutions, feasibility for a student prototype and impact, choosing the need with the highest score. To prove the need is real, we would gather CDC statistics on how many older adults fall and the costs of fall injuries, quotes from nurses about current bed alarms that cause alarm fatigue and our own observations, citing every source. Finally, we would identify the residents who benefit, the nurses who would use the innovation and the facilities that would purchase it, since their different needs will shape our design, training requirements and career implications.'},sources:[RULES,IDEO]};

export const MI3={title:'Originality, prior art and patents',hook:'An innovation that already exists cannot earn the 15 points for originality—no matter how well it is built. The guidelines make each team responsible for checking publications and patents. Learning to search prior art also makes you a better inventor: it shows what has been tried, what failed and where the gaps are.',objectives:['Explain originality and prior art.','Search patents and products effectively.','Differentiate your innovation from existing solutions.','Describe basic intellectual property concepts and ethics.'],sections:[
['What originality means',[
'An original innovation is your team’s **own idea and design** that is **meaningfully different** from what already exists. **Prior art** is any evidence that an idea is already known—patents, published articles, products on the market, conference presentations or even detailed online descriptions.',
'Originality does not require inventing an entirely new field. Many innovations **combine existing technologies in a new way** or **apply** a known technology to a new problem or population. What matters is a **novel, non-obvious** solution to the need you defined.',
'All artistic aspects of the exhibit must also be your original work or used in compliance with copyright law.',
"Judges often recognize common student ideas, such as basic pill reminders or simple fitness trackers. If your idea seems obvious, search especially carefully and look for a twist that makes it genuinely new.",
sort('Likely original or not original?',['Likely original','Not original'],[['Applying a known sensor in a new way to solve an unmet need',0],['Re-creating a product already sold online',1],['Combining technologies to solve a problem no one has addressed',0],['A design copied from a patent with minor color changes',1],['A new process for an under-served population, with no prior art found',0]])]],
['Searching for prior art',[
'Start with **broad internet searches** using several phrasings of the need and the solution. Then search **product sites**, medical supply catalogs, crowdfunding sites and app stores. Next, search **patent databases**: the **USPTO** patent search, **Google Patents** and the European Patent Office’s **Espacenet**. Finally, search **research databases** such as PubMed for published prototypes.',
'Use **keywords and synonyms** (fall, bed exit, nighttime mobility), and look at the **classifications** and **cited references** of similar patents to find related work.',
'Record every search—date, database, keywords and results—in your notebook as evidence of due diligence.',
"Search in several rounds. After the first round, use new words you found in similar patents and products to search again. Each round usually uncovers results the previous one missed.",
order('Prior art search',['Brainstorm keywords and synonyms','Search the web and product sites','Search app stores and catalogs','Search patent databases','Search research literature','Record all results in your notebook'])]],
['Reading a patent',[
'A patent document includes a **title**, **abstract** (summary), **drawings**, a **description** of the invention and **claims**—numbered statements that define exactly what is protected. Claims are the most important part legally; the abstract and drawings help you understand the idea quickly.',
'Patents may be **granted** (issued) or **applications** (pending). Even expired or abandoned patents count as prior art. Note the **filing date**, **inventors** and **assignee** (the company or person who owns it).',
'You do not need to understand every legal detail—focus on what the invention does and how it differs from your idea.',
"Drawings are often the fastest way to compare a patent with your idea. If the drawings show the same components arranged in the same way for the same purpose, your idea may not be original.",
match('Patent part and purpose',[['Abstract','Short summary of the invention'],['Claims','Define exactly what is protected'],['Drawings','Show how the invention looks or works'],['Description','Explains the invention in detail'],['Filing date','When the application was submitted'],['Assignee','Owner of the patent rights']])]],
['Differentiating your innovation',[
'After searching, create a **comparison table** of existing solutions and your idea. Compare features, cost, ease of use, who it serves and limitations. Your innovation should clearly address a **gap**: it might be cheaper, simpler, safer, more accessible or designed for a population others ignore.',
'If your search finds your exact idea, **pivot**—return to the need and brainstorm different solutions. Discovering this early saves months of work.',
'The comparison also helps you explain value and benefit to judges.',
"Your comparison table is also excellent evidence for the outline and presentation. It shows judges at a glance that you researched the field and that your innovation fills a specific gap.",
{t:'table',caption:'Comparison table (example invented for practice)',head:['Feature','Existing bed alarm','Pressure mat','Our innovation'],rows:[['Alerts before standing','No','No','Yes'],['Lights the path','No','No','Yes'],['False alarms','Frequent','Moderate','Designed to reduce'],['Estimated cost','Moderate','Low','Low']]}]],
['Intellectual property basics',[
'**Intellectual property (IP)** protects creations of the mind. A **patent** protects an invention for a limited time if it is new, useful and non-obvious. A **trademark** protects a brand name or logo. A **copyright** protects creative works such as writing, images and software code. **Trade secrets** are confidential business information.',
'Publicly sharing an invention can affect the ability to patent it in some countries, so teams interested in a patent should seek advice early. HOSA does not provide patent protection—that is the team’s responsibility.',
'You do not need a patent to compete.',
"Keep your own work records dated and signed in your notebook. Good records help show when and how your team developed the idea, which is useful if questions about originality ever arise.",
match('IP type and what it protects',[['Patent','A new, useful, non-obvious invention'],['Trademark','A brand name or logo'],['Copyright','Creative works such as images and text'],['Trade secret','Confidential business information'],['Public domain','Works free for anyone to use'],['License','Permission to use someone’s IP']])]],
['Ethics of innovation',[
'Ethical innovators **give credit**, never copy others’ designs and are honest about what their prototype can and cannot do. They consider **safety**, **privacy** (especially for apps and sensors that collect health data), **equity** (will it be affordable and accessible?) and **unintended consequences**.',
'Describe these considerations in your outline and presentation—they show judges a mature, realistic understanding of health care.',
"If your innovation uses artificial intelligence or algorithms, consider bias: a system trained on data from one population may not work as well for others. Mentioning this shows judges thoughtful, responsible design.",
chk('Your app collects heart rate data. Which ethical issue is most important to address?',['Privacy and security of health data','The app’s color scheme','The length of its name','Font choice'],'Protect sensitive data.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Prior art discovery',{a:{text:'Your search finds a patent that is very similar to your idea. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Study how it works, find a gap it does not address and pivot your design','b','Correct.',true],['Ignore it and continue','b','Originality is scored.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you document your search?',choices:[['Record dates, databases, keywords and findings in your notebook','c','Correct.',true],['Do not record it','c','Documentation shows due diligence.',false]]},c:{text:'Your redesigned innovation fills a clear gap.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Originality means your own novel, non-obvious solution; prior art includes patents, publications, products and online descriptions; combining or applying technologies in new ways can be original.','Search broadly—web, products, app stores, USPTO, Google Patents, Espacenet, PubMed—with keywords and synonyms; read abstracts, drawings and claims; record searches; build comparison tables and pivot if your idea exists.','IP includes patents, trademarks, copyrights and trade secrets; HOSA provides no patent protection; innovate ethically with credit, honesty, safety, privacy and equity.'],terms:[['Prior art','Existing evidence that an idea is already known.'],['Patent claims','Numbered statements defining what a patent protects.'],['Intellectual property','Legal protection for creations of the mind.']],mcqs:[q('What is prior art?','Existing evidence that an idea is already known','Artwork on the exhibit','A painting','The judges’ notes','Definition.'),q('Which database searches U.S. patents?','USPTO patent search','MedlinePlus','MyPlate','BLS','Patents.'),q('Which part of a patent defines what is protected?','Claims','Abstract','Title','Drawings','Legal scope.'),q('A trademark protects:','A brand name or logo','An invention','A trade secret','A song only','IP.'),q('If your search finds your exact idea, you should:','Pivot to a different solution','Copy it','Hide it','Ignore it','Originality.'),q('Who provides patent protection in this event?','The team, if it chooses to seek one','HOSA','The judges','No one can','Disclaimer.'),q('Combining existing technologies in a new way can be:','Original','Never original','Illegal','Not allowed','Innovation.'),q('Why record searches in a notebook?','To document due diligence','To fill pages','To impress friends','It is not useful','Evidence.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would make sure your medical innovation is original and how you would differentiate it from existing solutions.',rubric:[['Originality',['original','novel','non-obvious','prior art','combine','apply']],['Searching',['internet','products','uspto','google patents','espacenet','pubmed','keywords']],['Differentiation',['comparison','gap','pivot','claims','abstract','cheaper','safer']],['IP and ethics',['patent','trademark','copyright','hosa','credit','privacy','equity']]],model:'Our innovation must be our own novel, non-obvious solution, and anything already known—patents, publications, products or detailed online descriptions—counts as prior art. After defining our need of reducing nighttime falls in nursing homes, we would brainstorm keywords and synonyms such as fall prevention, bed exit, nighttime mobility and path lighting, then search the web, medical supply catalogs, crowdfunding sites and app stores, followed by the USPTO patent search, Google Patents, the European Patent Office’s Espacenet and PubMed for published prototypes. For each similar patent, we would read the abstract and drawings to understand it and the claims to see exactly what is protected, and follow its classifications and cited references to related work. We would record every search with the date, database, keywords and results in our notebook. Then we would build a comparison table of existing bed alarms, pressure mats and our idea across features, cost, ease of use, false alarms and populations served, showing the gap our design fills—such as alerting staff before a resident stands while lighting the path. If we found our exact idea, we would pivot back to the need and brainstorm new solutions. We would understand basic intellectual property—patents, trademarks, copyrights and trade secrets—and note that HOSA provides no patent protection. Finally, we would innovate ethically by crediting others, being honest about our prototype’s limits and addressing safety, privacy of any data and affordability.'},sources:[RULES,USPTO,EPO]};

export const MI4={title:'Designing the innovation',hook:'Once you know the need and the gaps, it is time to invent. Good design moves from many ideas to one strong concept, then defines exactly what the innovation must do. Judges look for innovations that are clearly described, well thought out and realistic—and that work for the people who will actually use them.',objectives:['Generate and select ideas using structured brainstorming.','Write design requirements and constraints.','Apply human factors and safety to the design.','Describe how the innovation works and how it is used.'],sections:[
['Brainstorming many ideas',[
'Begin with **quantity**: generate as many ideas as possible before judging them. Useful rules include **defer judgment**, **build on others’ ideas**, **encourage wild ideas** and **stay focused on the need**. Techniques include **sketching**, **mind maps**, “**How might we…?**” questions and borrowing ideas from other fields (biomimicry, consumer products, sports equipment).',
'Aim for dozens of rough ideas in a session. Many will be impractical, but they often spark the strongest concepts.',
'Keep all sketches in your notebook with dates.',
"Brainstorm individually before brainstorming together. Writing ideas alone for a few minutes prevents the loudest voice from shaping the whole session and produces a wider variety of ideas.",
sort('Good brainstorming practice?',['Good practice','Poor practice'],[['Building on a teammate’s idea',0],['Criticizing each idea immediately',1],['Encouraging wild ideas',0],['Stopping after the first idea',1],['Using “How might we…?” questions',0]])]],
['Selecting a concept',[
'Narrow ideas with a **decision matrix**: list criteria from your need and users—effectiveness, safety, cost, ease of use, feasibility to prototype, originality—and give each a **weight**. Score each concept and multiply by the weight to find the strongest option.',
'Discuss results as a team; a matrix supports judgment rather than replacing it. Sometimes combining two concepts produces the best design.',
"Choose criteria and weights before scoring concepts, not after. Deciding weights first keeps the team from adjusting them to favor a concept someone already likes.",
"Keep the losing concepts in your notebook. They show judges the range of ideas you considered and can become future improvements or backup plans if testing reveals problems.",
ex('Weighted decision matrix','Compare two concepts (scores 1–5; weights invented for practice).',['Criteria and weights: effectiveness 3, safety 3, cost 2, ease of use 2','Concept A scores: 4, 5, 3, 4 → 12 + 15 + 6 + 8 = 41','Concept B scores: 5, 3, 4, 3 → 15 + 9 + 8 + 6 = 38','Concept A has the higher weighted total'],'Concept A (41) beats Concept B (38) (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Design requirements and constraints',[
'**Design requirements** state what the innovation must do in measurable terms: “Alert staff within 5 seconds when a resident sits up,” “Light the path with at least a set brightness level,” “Be cleaned with standard hospital wipes.” **Constraints** are limits such as cost, size, power, materials and regulations.',
'Requirements guide prototyping and testing: each test checks whether a requirement is met. They also help you describe the innovation precisely in the outline.',
'Separate “must have” requirements from “nice to have” features.',
"Requirements should come from the need and users. If nurses told you alarms must be silent at night, “alert staff without sound in resident rooms” becomes a requirement rather than an afterthought.",
sort('Requirement or constraint?',['Requirement (what it must do)','Constraint (a limit)'],[['Alert staff within 5 seconds',0],['Cost under $50 to produce',1],['Fit under a standard bed',1],['Be cleanable with hospital wipes',0],['Run on batteries only for the exhibit',1],['Light the path to the bathroom',0]])]],
['Human factors and usability',[
'**Human factors engineering** designs products around human abilities and limitations, reducing errors. Consider users’ **strength**, **vision**, **memory**, **dexterity**, **stress** and **environment** (dim rooms, gloves, noise). Simple, intuitive designs with clear feedback (lights, sounds, labels) reduce mistakes.',
'For example, older adults may have reduced vision and grip strength, and nurses may be wearing gloves and multitasking. Designing for these realities makes the innovation safer and more likely to be adopted.',
'Usability testing with real or representative users reveals problems early.',
"Small details matter in human factors. Rounded edges, textured grips, color coding that works for people with color blindness and controls that are hard to press by accident can prevent errors.",
match('User factor and design response',[['Low vision','High-contrast, large labels'],['Weak grip','Large, easy-to-press buttons'],['Gloved hands','Controls that work with gloves'],['Noisy environment','Visual as well as audio alerts'],['Busy staff','Simple setup with few steps'],['Infection control','Smooth, cleanable surfaces']])]],
['Safety and risk',[
'Identify **risks**: What could go wrong? Could it injure someone, fail silently, cause a false sense of security or expose data? For each risk, describe a **mitigation**—rounded edges, fail-safe alarms, secure data storage, clear warnings.',
'Real medical devices are regulated by the **FDA** based on risk, from **Class I** (low risk) to **Class III** (high risk). Mentioning the likely class and safety testing your innovation would need shows judges realistic understanding.',
'Exhibit safety is also scored: no flames, body fluids, living organisms, sharps (even capped) or invasive procedures.',
"A simple risk table with columns for the risk, how likely it is, how serious it would be and the mitigation makes your safety thinking easy for judges to follow.",
chk('What is a mitigation?',['A design change or feature that reduces a risk','A type of patent','A marketing slogan','A cost estimate'],'Reduce risk.')]],
['Describing how it works and how it is used',[
'The outline’s largest section—up to **four slides**—explains **what the innovation is, what it does and how it is used**. Describe the **components**, **how they work together** and the **step-by-step use** from the user’s point of view. Diagrams, labeled sketches and photos of prototypes make the explanation clear.',
'Write for a reader who has never seen it: avoid jargon or define it, and show the innovation in context (in a patient’s room, in a clinic).',
"A simple labeled diagram can explain more than several paragraphs. Show the innovation’s parts with arrows and short labels, and use a numbered sequence to show how it is used.",
order('Describing use (example)',['Staff place the sensor pad under the mattress','Resident sits up at night','Pad detects weight shift','Path lights turn on softly','Staff receive a silent alert','Staff arrive to assist the resident'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Design decisions',{a:{text:'Your favorite concept is exciting but would require surgery to test. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Choose or adapt a concept that can be safely prototyped and demonstrated','b','Correct.',true],['Plan an invasive demonstration','b','Invasive procedures are prohibited.',false]]},b:{text:'Nurses say your alert is too loud and wakes other residents. How do you respond?',choices:[['Add a silent alert to staff devices and a soft visual cue','c','Correct.',true],['Ignore the feedback','c','Human factors matter.',false]]},c:{text:'Your design now meets user needs and safety requirements.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Brainstorm many ideas (defer judgment, build on ideas, “How might we…?”), then select with a weighted decision matrix based on effectiveness, safety, cost, ease of use and feasibility.','Write measurable design requirements and constraints; apply human factors (vision, grip, gloves, noise, busy staff, cleanability) and identify risks with mitigations; note FDA risk classes I–III.','Explain components, how they work and step-by-step use in up to four outline slides with diagrams and context.'],terms:[['Design requirement','A measurable statement of what an innovation must do.'],['Human factors engineering','Designing around human abilities and limitations to reduce errors.'],['Decision matrix','A table that scores options against weighted criteria.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a brainstorming rule?','Defer judgment','Criticize every idea','Stop at one idea','Avoid sketches','Quantity first.'),q('A weighted decision matrix helps:','Compare concepts against criteria','Write references','Search patents','Measure the exhibit','Selection.'),q('Which is a design requirement?','Alert staff within 5 seconds','Cost under $50','Fit under a bed','Battery only','Function.'),q('Which is a constraint?','Cost under $50 to produce','Alert staff quickly','Light the path','Be cleanable','Limit.'),q('Human factors engineering focuses on:','Designing around human abilities and limitations','Only appearance','Patent claims','Marketing','Usability.'),q('FDA Class III devices are:','Highest risk','Lowest risk','Not regulated','Toys','Risk classes.'),q('How many slides may describe the innovation and how it is used?','Up to 4','Up to 2','1','10','Outline.'),q('Which design helps users with low vision?','High-contrast, large labels','Tiny text','Dark gray on black','No labels','Accessibility.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would move from brainstorming to a well-defined innovation design.',rubric:[['Ideation',['brainstorm','defer judgment','how might we','sketch','many ideas']],['Selection',['decision matrix','criteria','weight','score','combine']],['Requirements and human factors',['requirements','constraints','measurable','human factors','vision','gloves','usability']],['Safety and description',['risk','mitigation','fda','class','components','step-by-step','diagram']]],model:'Starting from our need of reducing nighttime falls in nursing homes, we would hold brainstorming sessions using “How might we help residents get to the bathroom safely at night?” questions, deferring judgment, building on each other’s ideas, encouraging wild ideas and sketching dozens of concepts. We would narrow them with a weighted decision matrix scoring effectiveness and safety at weight 3 and cost and ease of use at weight 2, along with feasibility to prototype and originality, and discuss the results, perhaps combining two concepts into a sensor pad that triggers soft path lights and a silent staff alert. We would write measurable design requirements—detect a resident sitting up and alert staff within 5 seconds, light the path to the bathroom and be cleanable with standard hospital wipes—and constraints such as a production cost under $50, fitting under a standard bed and running on batteries. Applying human factors, we would design for residents with low vision and confusion and for nurses wearing gloves while multitasking, using simple setup, large controls and visual as well as silent alerts. We would list risks, such as a failed alert or tripping on cords, with mitigations like low-battery warnings and cordless design, and note that a real product would likely be an FDA-regulated device requiring safety testing. Finally, in up to four outline slides, we would describe the components, how they work together and step-by-step use in a resident’s room, with labeled diagrams and prototype photos.'},sources:[RULES,HF,FDA]};

export const MI5={title:'Prototyping and testing',hook:'A prototype turns your idea into something judges can see, touch and watch in action. In Round Two, the demonstration of your prototype is worth 15 points, and judges want to leave feeling competent about how to use it. Building and testing several versions is how good ideas become convincing innovations.',objectives:['Choose appropriate prototype fidelity for each stage.','Build prototypes safely within exhibit rules.','Test prototypes against requirements and with users.','Iterate and document improvements.'],sections:[
['What a prototype is for',[
'A **prototype** is a model built to **explore**, **test** or **demonstrate** an idea. Early prototypes answer questions quickly and cheaply (“Does this shape fit under a mattress?”). Later prototypes show how the innovation works and how it is used.',
'Your competition prototype does not need to be a finished product, but it should **clearly demonstrate the core function** and use. For digital innovations, a clickable mock-up or app prototype can serve the same purpose.',
'Judges score how well you demonstrate the prototype and whether it functions correctly.',
"Before building, decide what question each prototype should answer. A prototype built to check size does not need to work electronically, while a prototype for the demonstration must work reliably.",
"For digital innovations, a prototype might be a series of screen mock-ups that judges can click through on a battery-powered tablet, showing each step a user would take.",
chk('What should the competition prototype clearly show?',['The core function and how it is used','Every final manufacturing detail','Only the logo','A price tag'],'Demonstrate the key idea.')]],
['Levels of fidelity',[
'**Low-fidelity** prototypes—paper sketches, cardboard models, foam shapes, storyboards—test form and concept quickly. **Medium-fidelity** prototypes use 3D printing, craft materials or simple electronics to test function. **High-fidelity** prototypes look and work close to a final product.',
'Move from low to high fidelity as your design becomes clearer. Spending too long on a polished prototype too early can waste effort if the concept changes.',
'For apps and software, wireframes and clickable mock-ups are common low- to medium-fidelity tools.',
"Low-fidelity prototypes are also useful for early user feedback. People are often more willing to suggest changes to a rough cardboard model than to a polished device that looks finished.",
"Photograph each prototype from the same angles against a plain background. Consistent photos make it easy to show the evolution of your design on an exhibit panel or outline slide.",
order('Prototype progression',['Paper sketches and storyboards','Cardboard or foam model','3D-printed or craft functional model','Working demonstration prototype','Refined exhibit prototype'])]],
['Materials, tools and safety',[
'Common prototyping materials include **cardboard**, **foam**, **craft supplies**, **3D-printed parts**, **simple microcontrollers** (such as hobby boards), **sensors**, **LEDs** and **batteries**. Many schools and libraries have makerspaces with 3D printers and tools.',
'Follow safety rules: use tools with supervision, wear eye protection when cutting or drilling and handle batteries carefully. For the exhibit, **no electricity is provided**—power must come from **batteries**—and **flames, body fluids, living organisms, sharps (even capped or locked) and invasive procedures or skin puncturing** are prohibited.',
'Any sounds must not interfere with neighboring exhibits.',
"Plan your budget early. Prototyping supplies can add up, so ask your school, local businesses or makerspaces about donated materials and keep receipts for cost estimates.",
sort('Allowed in the exhibit prototype?',['Allowed','Not allowed'],[['A battery-powered sensor and LED',0],['A plug-in power strip',1],['A 3D-printed model',0],['A capped needle',1],['A foam model of a limb',0],['A demonstration that punctures skin',1]])]],
['Testing against requirements',[
'Test each **design requirement** with a clear method and record results. For “alert staff within 5 seconds,” time the alert in repeated trials. For “cleanable with hospital wipes,” wipe the surface repeatedly and check for damage. Use **simulations** and **models**, not real patients.',
'Report results honestly, including failures. Each failure tells you what to improve.',
"Record failures as carefully as successes. A failure log helps you explain to judges how testing shaped the final design, which demonstrates a thorough engineering process.",
ex('Recording a test','Test the alert time requirement (results invented for practice).',['Requirement: alert within 5 seconds','Method: teammate sits up on the pad 10 times; another times the alert','Results: 9 of 10 alerts in 2–4 seconds; 1 failure due to loose wire','Action: secure the wire and retest'],'90% success; fixing the wire becomes the next iteration (results invented for practice).')]],
['Testing with users',[
'**Usability testing** asks representative users to try the prototype while you observe. Ask them to complete tasks (“Set up the pad”) and to think aloud. Note where they hesitate or make errors, and ask what they like and dislike.',
'Test with classmates, teachers or, with permission, health professionals. Do not test on patients or use the prototype for real care. Simple surveys can collect ratings of ease of use.',
'User feedback is powerful evidence for your presentation.',
"After each usability test, ask one final question: “If you could change one thing, what would it be?” The answers often point directly to the most valuable next improvement.",
match('Testing method and what it reveals',[['Think-aloud test','Where users get confused'],['Timed task','How quickly users can complete setup'],['Ease-of-use rating','User satisfaction'],['Expert review','Clinical realism and safety concerns'],['Repeated trials','Reliability'],['Durability test','Whether it survives cleaning or handling']])]],
['Iterating and documenting',[
'**Iteration** means improving the design through repeated cycles of building, testing and refining. Each version should address problems found in testing. Keep **every version** and photograph it; showing the evolution of your prototype in the exhibit or presentation demonstrates a thorough process.',
'Document iterations in your notebook: date, version, change made, reason and test results. This record also supports originality.',
"Plan the final build early enough to leave time for at least two full rehearsals with the working prototype. Last-minute changes are a common cause of demonstration failures.",
order('Iteration cycle',['Build a version','Test against requirements and with users','Analyze results','Decide on improvements','Build the next version'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Prototype problems',{a:{text:'Two weeks before ILC, your electronic prototype fails intermittently. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the best plan?',choices:[['Fix and retest the weak point, and prepare a reliable backup demonstration model','b','Correct.',true],['Hope it works on the day','b','Plan for reliability.',false]]},b:{text:'You want to show the prototype working on a real patient. Is that appropriate?',choices:[['No—demonstrate with a model or teammate in a safe, non-invasive way','c','Correct.',true],['Yes, if the patient agrees','c','Use safe simulations.',false]]},c:{text:'Your demonstration is reliable, safe and clear.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Prototypes explore, test and demonstrate; the competition prototype must clearly show the core function and use (demonstration is worth 15 points in Round Two).','Progress from low fidelity (sketches, cardboard) to medium (3D prints, simple electronics) and high fidelity; follow tool safety and exhibit rules—battery power only; no flames, body fluids, living organisms, sharps or invasive procedures.','Test each requirement with repeated trials and usability tests with representative users (never real patients); iterate, keep and photograph each version and document changes and results.'],terms:[['Fidelity','How closely a prototype resembles the final product.'],['Usability testing','Observing users as they try a prototype.'],['Iteration','Improving a design through repeated build–test–refine cycles.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a low-fidelity prototype?','A cardboard model','A finished product','A manufactured device','A sold product','Quick and cheap.'),q('How must exhibit prototypes be powered?','Batteries only','Wall outlets','Generators','No power allowed at all','Rule.'),q('Which is prohibited in the exhibit?','A capped needle','A foam model','A 3D-printed part','An LED','Sharps.'),q('What does usability testing reveal?','Where users struggle','Patent status','Shipping cost','Exhibit width','Observation.'),q('Iteration means:','Repeated build–test–refine cycles','Building only once','Copying a product','Skipping tests','Improvement.'),q('Should prototypes be tested on real patients?','No—use simulations and representative users','Yes, always','Only on judges','Only in hospitals without permission','Safety.'),q('Why keep earlier prototype versions?','To show the design’s evolution','They are required by law','To fill the table','No reason','Process.'),q('A test showing 9 of 10 alerts on time has a success rate of:','90%','9%','50%','100%','Calculation.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would prototype and test your medical innovation and prepare a reliable demonstration.',rubric:[['Fidelity',['low-fidelity','cardboard','sketch','3d','medium','high-fidelity']],['Safety and rules',['battery','no electricity','sharps','flames','invasive','supervision']],['Testing',['requirements','trials','usability','think aloud','users','results']],['Iteration and demo',['iterate','versions','document','notebook','backup','reliable','demonstrate']]],model:'We would begin with low-fidelity prototypes—sketches, a storyboard of a resident’s night and a cardboard sensor pad—to check size and placement under a mattress, then build a medium-fidelity version with a 3D-printed housing, a pressure sensor, LEDs and a hobby microcontroller to test function, and finally a refined demonstration prototype for the exhibit. We would work in our school makerspace with supervision and eye protection, and design the exhibit version to run entirely on batteries, with no flames, body fluids, living organisms, sharps or invasive features, keeping sounds quiet for neighboring exhibits. We would test each design requirement: for alerting staff within 5 seconds, a teammate would sit up on the pad 10 times while another timed the alert; for cleanability, we would wipe the surface repeatedly with hospital-style wipes. We would also run usability tests with classmates and, with permission, a nurse, asking them to set up the pad while thinking aloud and rating ease of use, never testing on real patients. Each round of results would guide the next iteration—for example, securing a loose wire that caused one failure or enlarging a button that was hard to press with gloves. We would photograph and keep every version and record the date, change, reason and results in our notebook to show our process and originality. Before ILC, we would test the final prototype many times, carry spare batteries and parts and prepare a backup model so our 15-point demonstration is reliable and clear.'},sources:[RULES,IDEO]};
